        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Petra</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 400 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Nabataeans, a northern Arabian people once nomadic, who settled the site in the 4th century BCE and made it their capital in the 2nd century BCE; later Roman and Byzantine builders added roads, a colonnaded street and churches</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A city in a mountain basin reached through the narrow Siq gorge, with tombs, temples and monuments carved into rose-coloured sandstone in a Nabataean version of Hellenistic style, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> cut into the hillside, and dams, cisterns and conduits</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">In southern Jordan, on the eastern flank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Arabah</a> valley, managed since 2007 by the Petra Archaeological Park</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Treasury (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khazneh">Al-Khazneh</a>); the Monastery (Ad Deir); the Siq; rainwater collection and flood control; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade; the traditional tomb of Aaron; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1985</span></div>
