    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Nabataeans' control of water, through dams, cisterns and conduits, turned a desert basin into an artificial oasis and a hub of the caravan trade.</li><li>Annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 106, Petra declined as sea routes took its trade and after the earthquake of 363, though papyri show the city still flourishing in the 6th century.</li><li>Long abandoned except by a handful of nomads, it was described for Europeans by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> in 1812 and is now Jordan's most visited site.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The face of the Treasury is marked by hundreds of bullet holes, made by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> who hoped to dislodge riches once rumoured to be hidden inside it.</p></aside>
    </section>

