    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">4th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Nabataeans settled the Petra area</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Petra became the capital of the Nabataean Kingdom</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1st century</span><span class="rf-text">The city flourished, the Treasury was built and the population peaked at an estimated 20,000</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">106</span><span class="rf-text">Nabataea was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and became part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea">Arabia Petraea</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">363</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake destroyed many buildings and crippled the water management system</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">447</span><span class="rf-text">The Urn Tomb was consecrated as a church in the time of Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text">The Swiss traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> became the first European to describe the ruins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1985</span><span class="rf-text">Petra was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The area around Petra has been inhabited from as early as 7000 BCE, when some of the earliest recorded farmers settled at Beidha just to the north. Petra appears in Egyptian campaign accounts and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> as Pel, Sela or Seir, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, between 1200 and 600 BCE, the Edomites occupied the area, using its mountains as a reservoir of water and a stopping point for merchants trading wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and wood. An Edomite site on the summit of Umm el-Biyara dates from no earlier than the 7th century BCE.</p><p>The Nabataeans were one of several nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes of the Arabian Desert. Though at first embedded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> culture, they are identified by archaeological, religious and linguistic evidence as a northern Arabian tribe. They settled at Petra in the 4th century BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> describes the expeditions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> sent against them in 312 BCE, though his account implies no town yet existed there. Their own name for the city seems to have been Raqēmō; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> records that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of his day still called it Rekeme. Petra became the capital of the Nabataean Kingdom in the 2nd century BCE, its wealth drawn from its place on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade routes.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Petra&#8221; cites where it covers Bronze Age, Emergence of Petra, 19th and 20th centuries:</p><ul><li>Jane Taylor, Petra and the Lost Kingdom of the Nabataeans (I.B.Tauris, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Petra lies in a basin surrounded by mountains, next to Jabal Al-Madbah. Most visitors now enter from the east through the Siq, a dark gorge in places only 3–4 metres wide, formed by a split in the sandstone. At its end stands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khazneh">Al-Khazneh</a>, the Treasury, 24 metres wide and 37 metres tall, its façade carved with a broken pediment around a central tholos, the twins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Castor and Pollux</a>, and a female figure believed to be Isis-Tyche. Its style refers to the architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><p>Beyond lie a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> said to hold about 8,500 people, the Royal Tombs, among them the Palace, Corinthian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> and Urn Tombs, a pool and garden complex with an island pavilion, and the Colonnaded Street leading to the Sacred Quarter and its temples, the Qasr al-Bint and the Temple of the Winged Lions. The Monastery, Petra's largest monument at 45 metres tall and 50 metres wide, was dedicated to the deified king Obodas I. The High Place of Sacrifice on Jebel Madbah saw libations, animal sacrifice and the burning of frankincense. Most tombs hold small burial niches carved into the rock.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Petra&#8221; cites where it covers City centre:</p><ul><li>Bachmann, W., Watzinger, C. Wiegand, T., Petra, vol 3. Wissenschaftliche Vero¨ffentlichungen des Deutsch-Turkischen Denkmalschutz-Kommandos 3. (De Gruyter, 1921)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Excavations have shown that the Nabataeans' ability to control water led to the rise of the city. The area suffers flash floods, which they controlled with dams, cisterns and water conduits that stored water through long droughts and allowed the city to profit from its sale. The archaeologist Philip Hammond, who worked at Petra for nearly 40 years, believed the carved channels in the walls and ground once held ceramic pipes fed from rock-cut systems on the canyon rim.</p><p>The monuments were cut directly into the sandstone, their façades mostly in a Hellenistic style that reflects the many cultures, all influenced by Greek culture, with which the Nabataeans traded. The Monastery dates from the 1st century BCE, the Temple of the Winged Lions from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a> (9 BCE–40 CE), and the Treasury, possibly the mausoleum of Aretas IV, from the 1st century CE. New evidence suggests that prominent structures were aligned with sunrises and sunsets at the equinoxes and solstices. In 2016 satellite images and drones revealed a huge platform south of the city, tentatively dated to about 150 BCE, whose ceremonial purpose is unexplained.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> and other writers identified Petra as the capital of the Nabataeans and the centre of their caravan trade, controlling routes to Gaza, Bosra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The Nabataeans worshipped Arab gods, chief among them Dushara with Al-'Uzzā, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allat">Allat</a> and Manāt, and some of their deified kings. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed the kingdom in 106, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> built the Via Traiana Nova through the city, Hadrian visited in 130 and named it Hadriānī Petra Metropolis, and a governor raised a mausoleum for his son among the royal tombs.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> reached Petra in the 4th century, and the city became the capital of the Byzantine province of Palaestina III; the Urn Tomb was consecrated as a church in 447. A tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> struck the rock here to bring forth water, giving Wadi Musa its name, and that Aaron is buried on nearby Jabal Haroun, a belief going back at least to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>; a monastery there drew pilgrims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> held the area until 1189 and built castles, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan Baibars visited the ruins towards the end of the 13th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Petra declined as sea routes replaced the caravan trade and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> drew Arab trade away. The earthquake of 363 destroyed many buildings, yet 140 papyri found in the Byzantine Church, mostly contracts of the 530s to 590s, show the city still flourishing in the 6th century. It is not mentioned in accounts of the Muslim conquest, and by the early Islamic era it was abandoned except for a handful of nomads.</p><p>In 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a>, travelling in disguise, became the first European to describe it; artists such as David Roberts and Frederic Edwin Church followed. Weakened structures left tombs open to thieves. In the 1980s the Jordanian government resettled the Bedul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>, who had lived in the site's caves, at Umm Sayhoun. Petra became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1985, and conservation has addressed the Siq, the Treasury, temples, churches and wall paintings. The site faces collapse, flood erosion, salt weathering, poor restoration and heavy tourism, with 1.2 million visitors in 2019; animal welfare groups have documented the abuse of working animals.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has called Petra 'one of the most precious cultural properties of man's cultural heritage'. It is a symbol of Jordan and the country's most visited attraction, and in a popular poll in 2007 it was named one of the New 7 Wonders of the World. The skills and heritage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> of the area were inscribed on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage List in 2008.</p><p>The city has long held a place in writing and art. John William Burgon's prize poem of 1845 called it 'a rose-red city half as old as time', and it has appeared in novels such as Agatha Christie's Appointment with Death and the Tintin adventure The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> Sharks, in the Rahbani brothers' musical Petra, and in films including Indiana Jones and the Last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a>. For some Israeli youth of the 1950s, illicit hikes to Petra became a dangerous, sometimes deadly, rite of passage, remembered in the ballad 'The Red Rock'.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Petra, in southern Jordan, was settled by the Nabataeans in the 4th century BCE and grew into their capital and a hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade, sustained by careful control of water. After Roman annexation, earthquake and the loss of its trade it was abandoned, and since its description by Burckhardt in 1812 it has become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and Jordan's most visited place.</em></p>

